WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
24% a year of datacenter power growth still leaves supply behind compute demand. This is AI Chips &amp; Data Centres, episode 16. McKinsey's global energy perspective 2026 is the source via datacenter knowledge. Sam DeFabrizio, a partner there, says that from now to 2030, the most likely scenarios all have power supply lower than IT compute demand. The model watches monthly CPU and GPU purchase commitments.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:32.680
and hyperscaler capital spending announcements,

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.000
then test them against how fast power gear can actually show up.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.020
That delivery test includes interconnection approvals, permitting, moratoriums,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.500
transformer lead times, turbines, reciprocating engines, fuel cells,

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.640
and engineering procurement and construction capacity.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.100
More construction capacity, more turbine manufacturing,

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:59.680
and faster interconnection and permitting could let supply grow quicker than that baseline.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:05.480
Limited construction or efficiency gains from models from the buildings or from moving

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.560
when workloads draw power could pull electricity demand under it.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:15.100
Cheap generation does not carry electrification unless transmission, storage, and reliable

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.080
capacity are ready to deliver it.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:23.160
Operators who want power sooner are turning to gas engines and turbines, fuel cells and

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.080
battery storage.

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:31.480
OpenAI selected AMD epic-turned CPUs to host its jalapeno AI ASIC deployment, AMD

00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:33.760
says, pointing at Tom's hardware.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.660
The reasons OpenAI's team gave our platform strength, partner experience, and cutting

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:40.860
unnecessary risk.

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.800
That is a reported host CPU pick for an ASIC deployment, not a new merchant accelerator

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:47.480
on a shelf.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.960
Elastic Cloud is adding compute-optimized instances on ARM-based Microsoft Azure cobalt

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:57.260
for search workloads that burn CPU, ARM says.

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:01.240
Elastic reports up to 44% better price performance

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.640
on its CPU-optimized ARM profile.

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.300
That number is Elastic's price performance for the profile,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.260
not a training cluster result.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:11.780
It is a test of a desk side machine,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.460
not a fresh and video platform launch.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.640
Power limits are pushing European data center builds

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.080
past Frankfurt, London, Amsterdam, Paris, and Dublin.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:28.000
Grid access, construction capacity, and community support decide which of those announcements become halls that run.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.480
Data Center Knowledge reports from DC Bytes EMEA work.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.760
Oscar Gomez-Komelas, DC Bytes Research Manager for EMEA, calls power availability the principal obstacle

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.820
and says several steps have to land before an early stage project counts as realistic.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.340
The Nordics are bringing capacity online fastest because they started building earlier than

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:52.300
Malat and Madrid.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.260
Michael Winterson, Secretary-General of the European Data Center Association, expects

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:03.220
the list of European markets with more than one gigawatt installed to grow from 5 to

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:04.520
between 12 and 14.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.480
He ties that to national demand, lower risk on new deployments, and energy and planning

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:13.660
limits that push work toward markets that still have capacity.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:19.260
The old hubs still hold connectivity and customer demand, and a new market has to carry

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:21.800
a second project, not only a first campus.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:28.440
After Saturday's confirmation of early talks with TSMC, Elon Musk wrote on Wednesday that

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.740
his companies will build and run TerraFab.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.960
His words, we will build and run the fab, let there be zero doubt about that.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:41.860
Maybe TSMC sublices part of the TerraFab if they want, but nothing more than that.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:47.700
He was answering speculation that the likeliest deal had TSMC owning and running the fab,

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.000
with SpaceX investing or promising to buy chips.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:57.800
Intel Chief Executive Lip Bhutan told Bloomberg before a Tokyo event for 50 years of Intel

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.220
in Japan, that Intel will keep working on TerraFab.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:07.540
Tech Power Up reports the project wants to license Intel's 14A node and other technologies,

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:13.020
strategies. That Intel IP is the base for now, and that TSMC would not run capacity

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:19.080
itself. Knowledge help and a possible sublease, not a handoff of TSMC node IP.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.240
Benzinga also reports that Taiwan's semiconductor is planning to raise wafer foundry prices

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:32.620
by 3-6%. That is reported, not a price sheet TSMC published in this coverage. Take it apart

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.860
Art and a regional capacity total is four piles added together, not account of halls

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:38.560
that are on.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:47.120
Data Center Knowledge, citing DC Bite, puts EMEA IT capacity at 93 gigawatts across operating

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.140
sites, construction, committed projects, and early stage developments.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:56.960
Gomez Cometlas says roughly 60% of the region's pipeline is still early stage.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:01.760
An early stage gigawatts still needs power, a permit path, and a builder.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.660
It is not a transformer on order and not a hall that can take IT load.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.380
DCBite is describing a development landscape, not available supply.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.920
A buyer, a utility, and a lender are each reading a different line of that same stack.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.040
Winterson's EU-DCA forecast is another object.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.020
Roughly 15% compound annual growth over 7 years,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:28.800
taking deployed commercial capacity above 30 gigawatts.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:31.580
Deployed capacity is what is in service.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:37.180
The 93 gigawatt stack is what has been drawn, committed, started, or switched on.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.860
That stack loses its use as a reservation the moment the question is, which megawatts

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:43.620
can be energized?

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:49.320
Because an early stage gigawatt drops out until grid access, construction crews, and community

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:52.320
support turn it into a building that is actually on.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:59.260
On the horizon, January 2027 is the reported start month for that 3-6% wafer

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:04.400
foundry price rise. Within 12 months, DCBite expects the Nordic lead to

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:09.760
Nero as projects in Milan and Madrid become ready for service. That's AI chips

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.280
and data centers for today. I'm Patrick in Vancouver. Thanks for listening.

